From 16ac24b4ee567972d6bf2c615052933964d19a35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:37:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] gitattributes: pin .githooks to LF so the hook runs on Linux
 and macOS

Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which checks a shell script out with CRLF.
/bin/sh then fails on the shebang with a 'bad interpreter' error that does not obviously point
at line endings. The commit-msg hook added in the previous commit would have been broken for
anyone cloning on a POSIX system - caught by git's own CRLF warning at add time.

Same reasoning as the existing patches/** rule: files whose bytes are load-bearing do not get
to follow the cloner's text settings.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110D, public-release bar
Measured-effect: packaging only, no engine behaviour changed. Verified the file is stored with
  LF after re-adding under the new attribute.
---
 .gitattributes | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.gitattributes b/.gitattributes
index 8c563c5e4a..695b3ec9c5 100644
--- a/.gitattributes
+++ b/.gitattributes
@@ -13,3 +13,8 @@
 # `diff` is set alongside it so `-text` does not cost reviewability: git still renders these as
 # text in diffs instead of reporting "Binary files differ".
 patches/** -text diff
+
+# ArifiLabs: git hooks are shell scripts executed by /bin/sh. Checked out with CRLF they fail
+# on Linux and macOS with an unhelpful 'bad interpreter' error, and Git for Windows defaults
+# core.autocrlf=true - so pin them to LF regardless of the cloner's settings.
+.githooks/** text eol=lf
